<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.WebAssembly">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-browser</TargetFramework>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>browser-wasm</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <UseAppHost>false</UseAppHost>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <!-- Rask WASM marker: gates the framework's build/Rask.Wasm.targets (wwwroot staging +
         scoped-asset bake). Replaces the old WasmGenerateAppBundle marker. -->
    <RaskWasm>true</RaskWasm>
    <!--
      NOT prerendered yet — but the reason recorded here is no longer the reason, and the blocker it
      named is fixed. Read this before either turning it on or repeating the analysis.

      The objection was the MIXTURE. Only 5 of this app's 20 routes can be prerendered: the browser-API
      demos (media devices, fullscreen, serial, USB, HID, Bluetooth…) have nothing to bind to off a
      browser, throw during the pass, and are skipped by design. A static host answers an un-prerendered
      deep link with its SPA fallback, which is the root index.html — and once the root is prerendered
      that fallback stops being a neutral boot shell and becomes a fully rendered guides index, so
      deep-linking into one of the fifteen misroutes.

      That objection closed with its own stated prerequisite: "a fallback document that is still the boot
      shell rather than any one rendered page". #974 is exactly that — the pass now writes the untouched
      shell to 404.html before the page loop, so the fallback is neutral no matter what the root becomes.

      Measured on this app, publishing with -p:RaskPrerender=true:

        [Rask.Prerender] 20 route(s) to render, 3 skipped
        [Rask.Prerender] wrote the neutral boot shell to 404.html
        [Rask.Prerender] result written=5 skipped=3        (and 15 threw, as predicted)

      404.html came out at 8.7 KB with no guides markup in it; index.html at 239 KB fully rendered. So
      the mixture is safe now.

      Turning it on is therefore a judgement call about this sample rather than a blocked one, and it is
      left off here only because flipping it deserves its own browser-E2E run against a prerendered
      bundle — a prerendered document that the WASM runtime then morphs is a different first paint from
      the one every journey currently asserts.

      One real framework bug surfaced on the way and IS fixed: the prerender companion carried the app's
      references but not its global usings, so this app could not compile for net10.0 at all — sixty
      CS0103s on UiIconName, in files that build perfectly for the browser. See
      Rask.Wasm.Prerender.targets and PrerenderCompanionGenerationTests.
    -->
    <!-- <RaskPrerender>true</RaskPrerender> -->
    <!-- Fingerprint framework assets + fill the index.html import map / preload placeholders
         at publish (Microsoft.NET.Sdk.WebAssembly). Makes static-host redeploys cache-safe. -->
    <OverrideHtmlAssetPlaceholders>true</OverrideHtmlAssetPlaceholders>
    <!-- Full WASM AOT is opt-in: publish with -p:RaskWasmAot=true (needs the wasm-tools workload)
         to AOT-compile IL->WASM; the default keeps the Mono interpreter. Both are gated off the fast
         no-native build (-p:WasmBuildNative=false, used by the CI unit gate): the SDK records the
         runtime-pack default as empty, so even 'false' counts as a relink trigger that conflicts
         with WasmBuildNative=false. -->
    <RunAOTCompilation Condition=" '$(RaskWasmAot)' == 'true' ">true</RunAOTCompilation>
    <RunAOTCompilation Condition=" '$(RaskWasmAot)' != 'true' and '$(WasmBuildNative)' != 'false' ">false</RunAOTCompilation>
    <!-- Continuously enforce AOT-safety on every build (not just at publish): the trim + AOT Roslyn
         analysers run under warnings-as-errors, so a new RequiresDynamicCode/trim hazard fails the
         build long before the slow emscripten relink. -->
    <EnableAotAnalyzer>true</EnableAotAnalyzer>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    <!-- Trimming is safe: page types reach the runtime via the route registry's generated
         module initialiser, which emits [DynamicDependency] per registered page; the reflection
         sites in RouteChainRenderer / PageBinder / RouteValueParser are either DAM-annotated or
         carry a documented suppression. -->
    <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
    <TrimMode>full</TrimMode>
    <!-- This app hosts the guides, and the localization guide's demo formats the same values across
         four languages — which needs real culture data. So it ships ICU (~2.6 MB of icudt*.dat under
         _framework/) rather than dropping it. One property covers both halves: it also clears
         PredefinedCulturesOnly, which otherwise defaults to true under invariant globalization and
         makes CultureInfo.GetCultureInfo("hu-HU") THROW rather than fall back.

         This also makes the showcase the repo's ICU canary: the opt-in is exercised by the browser
         E2E gate on every push rather than only by an MSBuild property assertion. -->
    <RaskGlobalization>true</RaskGlobalization>
    <!-- Suppress IL2104 from Microsoft.JSInterop. The underlying warnings (IL2065 / IL2111)
         live in DotNetDispatcher.ScanAssemblyForCallableMethods — Blazor's reflection-driven
         [JSInvokable] scanner. Rask apps that only INVOKE JS (the common case) never trigger
         that path. WASM apps that DO mark methods [JSInvokable] should add a
         [DynamicDependency] on those methods (standard Blazor WASM mitigation). -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);IL2104</NoWarn>
    <!--
      This app has scoped TypeScript (PictureInPictureDemo.ts) AND islands, and a Lit island is
      spelled exactly like a scoped file: `Name.ts` beside `Name.cs`. Left on, convention-pairing
      offers every scoped file to the bundler as a Lit island and the build dies on a module that
      never default-exported a tag name. There are no Lit islands here — the Server showcase
      carries that case — so turning the pairing off costs nothing and says what is true.
    -->
    <RaskExternalLitAutoPair>false</RaskExternalLitAutoPair>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The one reference. On net10.0-browser this resolves to the WebAssembly host plus the trim-safe
         batteries; the same PackageReference on a net10.0 project resolves to the ASP.NET host plus the
         server batteries. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Rask\Rask.csproj"/>

    <!-- A real consumer gets this from the package's own build/Rask.props. In-repo we reference the
         PROJECT, and a ProjectReference does not import a package's build props, so the same rooting is
         spelled out here. Without it the trimmer drops Rask.dll — nothing references it statically, since
         its wiring is handed to the host from a [ModuleInitializer] — and the batteries silently do
         nothing. Measured: Rask.dll and Rask.Query were absent from the published bundle entirely. -->
    <TrimmerRootAssembly Include="Rask"/>
    <!-- The islands showcase. Same front-end files as the Server sample, bundled here because the
         client runtime resolves the manifest app-rooted at /_rask/external/manifest.json. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Rask.External\Rask.External.csproj"/>
    <!-- Arrived with the guides and demos when the Shared library folded into this project. Rask.Core
         and Rask.Html are named explicitly rather than left to the Rask meta-package: the library used
         to carry them, and the meta reference does not flow them to the compiler — dropping them takes
         `Component` itself out of scope, which arrives as ~1,900 errors that all look like missing
         usings. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Rask.Core\Rask.Core.csproj"/>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Rask.Html\Rask.Html.csproj"/>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Rask.Ui\Rask.Ui.csproj"/>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Rask.Validation.FluentValidation\Rask.Validation.FluentValidation.csproj"/>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Rask.Cqrs\Rask.Cqrs.csproj"/>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Rask.Batteries.Generators\Rask.Batteries.Generators.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"/>
    <!-- Build-order edge only: puts Rask.Tailwind.Tasks.dll on disk before the UsingTask that loads it
         runs. Build-only, so the payload is unchanged. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Rask.Tailwind\Rask.Tailwind.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      PrivateAssets="all"
                      SkipGetTargetFrameworkProperties="true"/>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Rask.Generators\Rask.Generators.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"/>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Vendored third-party assets (Bootstrap, Bootstrap Icons + fonts, Highlight.js), the
       placeholder image, and the HTTP-demo JSON fixture live under wwwroot/ and are
       discovered as static web assets by Microsoft.NET.Sdk.WebAssembly automatically,
       served at /lib, /img, /data. The framework's build targets additionally stage
       index.html / main.js / rask.wasm.js into wwwroot (see build/Rask.Wasm.targets). -->

  <!-- Embed the WASM-only demo sources verbatim so CodeSample can show the real file text
       beside the live Result, exactly like Rask.Site — these demos can't live in
       the shared project (they reference Rask.Wasm.Browser). EmbeddedSource reads them as
       raksrc/{leaf}; Program.cs registers this app assembly with EmbeddedSource so the lookup
       finds them. A .cs stays compiled too; this only adds its text as a manifest resource. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Features/**/*.cs;Features/**/*.ts;Features/**/*.js;Features/**/*.css;Features/**/*.tsx;Features/**/*.vue;Features/**/*.svelte">
      <LogicalName>raksrc/%(Filename)%(Extension)</LogicalName>
    </EmbeddedResource>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Guard the bare-leaf LogicalName: a duplicate leaf would silently overwrite another in
       the manifest (last writer wins), so EmbeddedSource would show the wrong file. Only the
       raksrc/ items are checked, so unrelated SDK EmbeddedResource items don't trip it. -->
  <Target Name="VerifyUniqueEmbeddedSourceNames" BeforeTargets="CoreCompile">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_RaskEmbeddedLeaf Include="@(EmbeddedResource->'%(LogicalName)')"
                         Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(LogicalName)').StartsWith('raksrc/'))"/>
    </ItemGroup>
    <Error Condition="'@(_RaskEmbeddedLeaf->Distinct()->Count())' != '@(_RaskEmbeddedLeaf->Count())'"
           Text="Duplicate embedded-source leaf name(s) under Features/. EmbeddedSource resolves by bare filename (raksrc/{leaf}), so every embedded .cs/.js/.css must have a unique file name across the project (and not collide with a Rask.Site leaf). Rename the collision." />
  </Target>

  <!-- The kit's namespace, project-wide. Its icons and components are named in markup all over this
       project, and a per-file using on ~60 files would be noise. Mirrors how Rask.Html imports
       Rask.Core for the same reason. -->
  <!--
    The islands' front-end files. Styles/app.css names the same directory in `@source`, and that is a
    TAILWIND-side path — nothing hands it to MSBuild, so without this the sheet is built from them once
    and then never rebuilt when one changes.

    The two have to be stated together: `@source` decides what Tailwind SCANS, this decides what the
    build WATCHES, and a file in one but not the other goes stale silently.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <RaskTailwindSource Include="Features/Islands/**/*.vue;Features/Islands/**/*.tsx;Features/Islands/**/*.svelte;Features/Islands/**/*.ts"/>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="ColorCode.HTML"/>
    <PackageReference Include="Markdig"/>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Rask.Site.Tests"/>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- The repo's user-facing guides, embedded so the site can render them on-site. GuideContent reads
       them as raskdoc/{leaf} and renders with Markdig at render time (cached). The bare leaf is the
       slug: raskdoc/routing.md -> /guides/routing. Recursive, so tutorial/, apis/ and architecture/ are
       surfaced too; leaf names are unique across docs/ and VerifyUniqueEmbeddedSourceNames below fails
       the build if that ever breaks. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\docs\**\*.md">
      <LogicalName>raskdoc/%(Filename)%(Extension)</LogicalName>
      <Link>Docs/%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</Link>
    </EmbeddedResource>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- The guides' own demo sources, alongside the Features/ ones already embedded above. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Shared/**/*.cs;Shared/**/*.ts;Shared/**/*.css">
      <LogicalName>raksrc/%(Filename)%(Extension)</LogicalName>
    </EmbeddedResource>
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Take the kit's stylesheet as a cached FILE rather than inlining it; linked in App.HeadAssets
         with UiStylesheet.Href(). Opt-in, because referencing the kit is not the same as wanting a
         file in wwwroot. -->
    <RaskUiWriteStylesheet>true</RaskUiWriteStylesheet>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Rask.Ui"/>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
